# Reconciliator design

How a program of the Fearless family makes the desktop of the system open a set of extensions with that program, and show the icons of those extensions and of the windows of that program.
The manager (see ControllersDesign) and the portable Fearless call the Reconciliator. The Reconciliator knows nothing of projects and mains: the caller gives the extensions, the icons and the windows.

### The data

The 'identity' of a program is the file name of the launcher of that program, without extension.
The 'Fearless family' is the set of the identities holding the text `earless`.
Each version of the manager and each version of the portable Fearless has an identity of its own.
The program folder of a manager holds two launchers, `<name>` and `<name>w`: they are two members of the Fearless family, with the identities `<name>` and `<name>w`, and one manager folder.
The kind of an extension follows from the text of the extension: `.fearless`, `.fapp` and three digits, and `.ffile` and three digits are Fearless extensions; every other extension is a system extension.
```
ExtensionIcon: { .extension: FileExtension; .icon: FsPath }    // the .ico on Windows, the PNG on Linux and macOS
WindowIcon: { .windowClass: Str; .name: Str; .target: FsPath; .png: FsPath }  // Linux only
Reconciliator: {
  mut .reconcile(sys: mut System, identity: Str, command: FsPath, extensions: List[ExtensionIcon], windows: List[WindowIcon], programIcon: FsPath, replaced: mut MF[FsPath, Void]): Void;
  mut .eradicateAll(sys: mut System): Void;      // deletes the setup of every identity of the Fearless family, with no check
  }
```
`.reconcile` makes `command` the program opening exactly `extensions`, under `identity`, each extension with its icon, and installs the icons of `windows`.
`programIcon` is the icon of `command` itself: the `.ico` on Windows, the PNG on Linux and macOS.
The 'setup' of an identity is every registry key and file `.reconcile` writes for that identity.
The setup of every other identity of the Fearless family, and the setup of `identity` when that setup has another command, is deleted, after `replaced` is called on the command of that setup. So only one member of the Fearless family at a time opens `.fearless`, and a program that takes `.fearless` from another member of the Fearless family releases every extension of that other member.
`.reconcile` takes the whole wanted set at once. Reconciling an unchanged set changes no setup.
Every `.reconcile` that passes its checks ends with one refresh of the desktop (`SHChangeNotify` on Windows, the MIME and desktop databases on Linux, `LSRegisterURL` of the 'helper bundle' on macOS), also when no setup changed: a changed image at the same path then shows too.
The checks of `.reconcile` change nothing when the checks fail, so the previous setup stays in place.
An extension left out of a `.reconcile` loses the setup of the identity only, and the previous program opens the files of that extension again.

### Refusals

- `.fearless` belongs to the Fearless family alone. A program outside the Fearless family opening `.fearless`, or the own choice of the user of a program for `.fearless` (a Windows UserChoice, even when the UserChoice names this identity), makes `.reconcile` refuse `.fearless`.
- A Fearless extension other than `.fearless` gets the very same setup as `.fearless`, and is checked like `.fearless`. Fearless extensions are invented by Fearless, so a Fearless extension set up in any other way than the setup of the Fearless family was tampered with.
- A system extension gets the same setup as `.fearless` too, but `.reconcile` takes a system extension over from whatever program opens the extension: the user allowed the extension on purpose, so other programs registered for the extension do not make `.reconcile` refuse the extension. A Windows UserChoice still does, as for `.fearless`: the user pinned that extension to a program, and `.reconcile` does not undo the own choice of the user.
More than one identity of the Fearless family already on the system makes `.reconcile` refuse.
A refusal raises an error holding each refused extension with the programs holding that extension, or, for more than one identity of the Fearless family, the list of those identities.

### Extensions on Linux

On Linux a program opens MIME types, not extensions; the shared MIME database turns a file name into a type through name patterns (globs). Several extensions can share one type (`*.htm` and `*.html` are both `text/html`), so taking over a type would take over all the extensions of that type. `.reconcile` never takes over a type: `.reconcile` gives each extension a type of its own, in the MIME package of the identity (the file `$XDG_DATA_HOME/mime/packages/<identity>.xml`, which only `.reconcile` writes):
```
<mime-type type="application/x-fearless-htm">
  <glob pattern="*.htm" weight="100"/>
  <sub-class-of type="text/html"/>
  <sub-class-of type="application/xhtml+xml"/>
</mime-type>
```
- The glob weight is 100, above every glob shipped by the systems we target (at most 80), so the name `x.htm` turns into the type of the identity and `x.html` stays `text/html`.
- When a system glob also matches the name, GIO and Qt look at the file content to choose, and text content would go to the system type. The `sub-class-of` entries, one for each type the system database gives to `*.htm`, make the content of those types count as the type of the identity. A file whose content is unmistakably another format (a `.htm` file starting with `<?php`) still gets that other type; this is accepted.
- The `.desktop` of the identity lists the types of the identity; no other program lists those types, so a double click on a `.htm` file reaches `command`, and every other program opening `text/html` stays in the "open with" choices.
- `.fearless` keeps its type `application/x-fearless`. A Fearless extension gets `application/x-fearless-<ext>` like a system extension; no system glob can match it.
- Nothing outside the files of the identity changes. Releasing an extension rewrites the MIME package of the identity without that extension, and the system types are back as they were.

### Extensions on macOS

On macOS a program opens types, not extensions: an application declares, in the `Info.plist` of the application, the types the application opens, and the extensions of the types the application invents.
Changing the `Info.plist` of a signed application breaks the signature of that application.
So the application of `command` declares `.fearless` when that application is built, and `.reconcile` declares every other extension in the 'helper bundle' of the identity.
The 'helper bundle' of an identity is the application `~/Library/Application Support/fearlessAssociations/<identity>.app`, which only `.reconcile` writes: an `Info.plist`, a launcher script running `command` with the arguments of the launcher script, the icon of each extension and the icon of `command`.
The 'helper bundle' is the setup of the identity: the identities on the system are the 'helper bundles' in `fearlessAssociations`, and `.reconcile` writes the 'helper bundle' also when `.fearless` is the only extension.
The 'declared type' of an extension is the first type macOS gives for that extension that an application outside `fearlessAssociations` declares.
- An extension with no 'declared type' gets a type of its own: `<identity>.<ext>`, with `-` for each `_` and in lower case, because a type name holds no `_` and macOS reads type names in lower case. The 'helper bundle' declares that type and opens that type as owner (`LSHandlerRank` `Owner`), so a double click on a file of that extension reaches `command`.
- A Fearless extension other than `.fearless` with a 'declared type' makes `.reconcile` refuse that extension; the program holding that extension is the application declaring the 'declared type'.
- A system extension with a 'declared type' gets no new type: the 'helper bundle' opens the 'declared type' as alternate (`LSHandlerRank` `Alternate`). `command` is then in the "Open With" choices of every extension of the 'declared type' (of `.html` too when the extension is `.htm`), and the program opening the 'declared type' stays the same.
- When an application outside `/System` declares the 'declared type', and the previous `Info.plist` of the 'helper bundle' does not name the 'declared type', `.reconcile` asks macOS once to make the 'helper bundle' the program opening the 'declared type'; macOS asks the user to confirm.
- `.reconcile` does not ask for a 'declared type' that an application in `/System` declares: for `public.html` macOS would ask the user to change the default web browser.
- A double click on a file runs the launcher script of the 'helper bundle', so `command` starts with zero arguments, and macOS passes the file as an event (see Starting the 'single' manager in ControllersDesign).
- `.reconcile` checks nothing for `.fearless`. The application of every installed program of the Fearless family declares `.fearless`: with two of those applications installed, macOS chooses which application opens a `.fearless` file.
- Deleting the setup of an identity registers the 'helper bundle' with an `Info.plist` declaring no type, then deletes the 'helper bundle'.

### Icons on the desktop

On Windows the registry names an icon by its path, so the desktop reads the `.icon` of each `ExtensionIcon` from that path. On Linux a MIME type names its icon, and the desktop looks names up only in icon theme folders: `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons` of the user, `$XDG_DATA_DIRS/icons` of the system (root only) and the legacy `~/.icons`. `.reconcile` installs each icon as `$XDG_DATA_HOME/icons/hicolor/256x256/mimetypes/<identity>-<hash>.png`; the hash gives a changed image a new name, past the icon cache of the desktop. Every file there named `<identity>-<hash>.png` belongs to that identity: each `.reconcile` deletes the files of that identity not in the wanted set, and deleting the setup of an identity deletes all the files of that identity together with the `apps/<identity>.png`, the `.desktop` files and the MIME package of that identity.
On Linux each window class of `windows` gets a `.desktop` file with `NoDisplay=true`, holding `.name` as its name, `command` followed by `.target` as its command, the installed icon as its icon and the window class as its `StartupWMClass`: GNOME and COSMIC show that icon for every window of that window class.
On macOS the `Info.plist` of the 'helper bundle' names an icon file for each type: `.reconcile` writes the icon of each extension as `Contents/Resources/<ext>.icns`, an icns file holding the PNG scaled to 256 pixels. Finder shows the icon of a new extension at once. After the icon of an extension changes, Finder keeps showing the first icon of that extension until the icon store of macOS is rebuilt.

### Notes

- The code of the Reconciliator is today the package `fileAssociations` of Commons (`FileAssociations`, `WindowsAssociations`, `LinuxAssociations`, `MacAssociations`, `Ico`, `Icon`, `WindowIcon`, `Shell`, `Xdg`), called by `Controllers/src/controller/Association.java` and by `offerAssociation` of `Coordinator/src/mainCoordinator/Main.java`. That code becomes a component of its own, called from the repositories Controllers and Coordinator.
- The exact checks and steps of `.reconcile` are those of the doc comment of `FileAssociations` in Commons.
- `Ico` makes the `.ico` of an icon from the PNG of the icon; `._writeIcons` of CoordinatorDesign and the manager (Conflict icon in ControllersDesign) need `Ico`.
- The code passes both an `.ico` and a PNG for each extension and for the program (`Icon`, `programIco`, `programPng`), where the design passes only the file the system reads.
- A `.reconcile` that finds no setup of the identity and is given no extension changes nothing and refreshes nothing: on Linux the tools refreshing the MIME and desktop databases fail on folders that do not exist.
